{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786663345,"nonce":"fCQMzMUoXUaMddL/PmhjyqN38yc=","title":"test/harness's fixed `Epoch` drifts out of `oto_partitions_manage()`'s live partition window, so a DB-backed timeline write fails a nameless 23514 that reads as a generic database defect","message":"**What is wrong.** `test/harness/postgres.go:40` pins every harness `FakeClock` at a fixed instant — `var Epoch = time.Date(2026, 1, 1, 12, 0, 0, 0, time.UTC)` — with the comment at `:37-39` explaining why fixed: \"so that a test which advances the clock can state its expectations as arithmetic rather than as 'roughly now'.\" But `alert_events` is RANGE-partitioned monthly on `recorded_at`, and the one function that creates those partitions tracks the real clock, not `Epoch`: `db/migrations/00005_partitions.sql:202-206` runs `oto_ensure_partitions_ahead('alert_events', 'month', 3, v_now)` where `v_now := now()` (`:169`), i.e. \"this month + next 3\" (`:202`) of the DATABASE's wall-clock month. `test/harness/postgres.go:265` calls this once per template bootstrap (`SELECT oto_partitions_manage()`), and its own comment at `:260-264` states the design: \"`alert_events`, `ingest_batches`, `ingest_rejections` and `ui_events` are partitioned with NO default partition, deliberately: a row outside every range must fail loudly rather than pile up in a bucket nobody can drop.\" The same sentence is the function's own contract, verbatim, in `db/migrations/00036_retention_defaults.sql:92`: \"There are deliberately NO DEFAULT partitions: a row that falls outside every range must fail loudly rather than silently accumulate in a bucket that can never be dropped.\" As of this writing the current month is 2026-08, so a template bootstrap today creates exactly four partitions — `alert_events_p202608`, `p202609`, `p202610`, `p202611` (naming per `db/migrations/00005_partitions.sql:10`, `SELECT p_ahead` loop at `:101` runs `i IN 0..3` inclusive) — none of which contains `Epoch`'s month, 2026-01. Any DB-backed test that appends a timeline event stamped at `Epoch` therefore lands in no partition, and the gap between `Epoch` and the live window only widens: it was seven months on 2026-08-14 and grows by one every month `Epoch` is not moved.\n\n**The demonstration.** Postgres refuses the row with SQLSTATE 23514 (\"no partition of relation \\\"alert_events\\\" found for row\"), a check_violation that names no constraint — there is no CHECK to name, the row simply matches no partition's range. `internal/platform/db/sqlstate.go:98` maps `23514` unconditionally to `{kind: errs.KindInternal, message: \"a row violated a database constraint\", namesConstraint: true}`, and `constraintCode` (`:257-267`) falls back to `\"sqlstate_\" + pg.Code` whenever `pg.ConstraintName` is empty and the caller's `ErrorPolicy.Codes` names no override for `23514` — which the alerts repository's policy does not (`internal/alerts/repository/mapper.go:48-53` sets only `NotFound`/`QueryFailed`, no `Codes` map). So the error a test or an operator actually sees is `errs.Error{Kind: KindInternal, Code: \"sqlstate_23514\", Message: \"a row violated a database constraint\"}` — a 500 that names no table, no column and no partition, for a defect that is entirely \"the harness's clock and the database's partitions disagree about what month it is.\"\n\n**Why it matters.** This is not a hypothetical trap; it has already been independently rediscovered twice, and each time the fix was local rather than structural. `internal/app/timeline_events_db_test.go:79-90` builds its rig's clock as `clock.NewFake(time.Now().UTC())` instead of `harness.Epoch`, with a comment naming the exact mechanism: \"NOT harness.Epoch, AND THE REASON IS THE PARTITION MANAGER... `alert_events` has no default partition on purpose, so an append stamped at Epoch fails with 'no partition of relation' the moment real time leaves that month.\" `internal/grouping/service/fanout_test.go:197-218` adds a dedicated `partitionedNow()` helper returning `time.Now().UTC()`, with a near-identical comment block reaching the same conclusion, added in commit `0b19564` (\"fix(grouping): a group fan-out has a ceiling, and says when it hits one\") — not `39ad13a`, which is an unrelated rules-drift fix. Both comments explicitly flag the other as a sibling doing the same workaround for the same reason. Two independent authors hitting the same trap and each patching around it locally, rather than at the harness, is the signature of a defect class that will be rediscovered a third time — and unlike most such traps, this one gets strictly worse with the calendar: every month that passes without moving `Epoch` widens the gap between the fixed instant and the live four-month partition window, so a workaround that derives `now()` at test-write time is doing the harness's job by hand, per call site, forever.\n\nMeanwhile a wide swath of other DB-backed tests do the opposite and rely on `Epoch` being an exact, fixed value: `internal/enrichment/repository/cache_test.go:84` asserts `got.ComputedAt().Equal(harness.Epoch)`; `internal/enrichment/repository/enrichments_test.go:46-47` stores `ComputedAt: harness.Epoch`; `internal/sources/repository/clusters_clock_test.go:151` and `internal/notification/repository/clock_test.go:123` assert deletion timestamps as `harness.Epoch.Add(-lag)`; `internal/channels/repository/channels_clock_test.go:28-30` calls out that the skew being months-wide, not seconds-wide, is deliberate (\"MONTHS behind the container's wall clock. That is the point\"). None of these touch a partitioned table, so none of them hit the 23514 — but it means `Epoch` cannot simply be redefined as `time.Now()` without breaking a second, larger set of tests that depend on it staying put.\n\n**Where.** `test/harness/postgres.go:37-40` (`Epoch`'s definition and rationale), `:260-265` (`oto_partitions_manage()` call and the no-default-partition comment); `db/migrations/00005_partitions.sql:165-206` (`oto_partitions_manage`, \"this month + next 3\" at `:202-206`), `:217` and `db/migrations/00036_retention_defaults.sql:92` (the binding \"NO DEFAULT partitions\" comment, verbatim); `internal/platform/db/sqlstate.go:98` (the `23514` row), `:257-267` (`constraintCode`'s fallback to `sqlstate_\u003ccode\u003e`); `internal/alerts/repository/mapper.go:48-53` (no `Codes` override for `23514` on this path); `internal/app/timeline_events_db_test.go:79-90` and `internal/grouping/service/fanout_test.go:197-218` (the two independent workarounds, the latter from commit `0b19564`); `internal/enrichment/repository/cache_test.go:84`, `enrichments_test.go:46-47`, `internal/sources/repository/clusters_clock_test.go:151`, `internal/notification/repository/clock_test.go:123` (tests that depend on `Epoch` staying fixed).\n\n**Scope.** medium — no source file changes with production consequences, but the fix touches shared test infrastructure (`test/harness`) that every DB-backed test depends on, and the candidate fixes are not interchangeable: a wrong choice either reintroduces the trap or silently breaks the fixed-timestamp assertions listed above.\n\n**Done when.** One of the following is chosen and applied, deliberately rather than by default: (a) the harness template bootstrap calls `oto_partitions_manage()` again around `Epoch` specifically (e.g. `oto_ensure_partition('alert_events', 'month', Epoch)`), so `Epoch`'s own month always has a partition regardless of wall-clock drift, leaving `Epoch` itself untouched for the tests that assert it as a fixed value; or (b) a documented, test-schema-only default partition is added, with an explicit note that production (per `db/migrations/00036_retention_defaults.sql:92`) deliberately has none and why a test-only divergence from that rule is acceptable; or (c) `Epoch` is redefined to track `now()`, with every fixed-timestamp assertion enumerated above updated or confirmed compatible. Whichever is chosen, `internal/app/timeline_events_db_test.go`'s derived-`now` rig and `fanout_test.go`'s `partitionedNow()` helper are revisited so the fix lives once, at the harness, rather than three times at call sites — and a comment at the chosen fix site names this ticket so a fourth rediscovery finds the answer instead of writing a third workaround.","files":null}]}